WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:04.070
Not even death could keep Picard from coming
back for season 2.

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:08.850
The "Star Trek" universe continues to boldly
go where no show has gone before — sort

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:14.900
of — as it follows Jean-Luc Picard years
after his adventures on the Starship Enterprise.

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:19.910
The first season sees the former admiral investigate
the truth behind Data's daughter and a new

00:00:19.910 --> 00:00:21.829
breed of synthetic beings.

00:00:21.829 --> 00:00:26.109
His mission takes him across the universe,
even revisiting his time with the Borg, while

00:00:26.109 --> 00:00:29.039
also dealing with a sinister Romulan plot.

00:00:29.039 --> 00:00:33.690
There's also the small problem that Picard
is dying of Irumodic Syndrome, a degenerative

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:36.190
condition that slowly robs him of his memory.

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:41.620
It's an action-packed season that ends as
Dr. Agnes Jurati transfers Picard's consciousness

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:43.660
into a synthetic body.

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:45.719
Don't worry — this doesn't mean he's immortal.

00:00:45.719 --> 00:00:50.610
The body has been programmed to expire around
the same time Picard would've died naturally.

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:52.140
So that's ... good?

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:56.980
Anyway, from there, the universe is Picard's
oyster since he has the crew of La Sirena

00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:58.980
to go on more adventures with.

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:03.839
But "Picard" Season 2 will up the stakes for
the show's sophomore season, with the extra-dimensional

00:01:03.839 --> 00:01:10.250
being known as Q returning to break the universe
as one final task for the series' namesake hero.

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:14.760
The season throws Picard and his crew back
into the 21st century to see how they save

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:19.600
the future — if anyone can fix the fabric
of reality, it's the former captain of the

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:21.340
Enterprise, that's for sure.

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:24.620
But what do fans need to know about "Picard"
Season 2?

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:28.550
First things first: The good news is that
"Picard" Season 2 is heading to our screens

00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:29.550
very soon.

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:32.380
It will arrive on Paramount+ on March 3rd.

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:37.180
There's a total of 10 episodes this season,
so it's keeping the story quite tight compared

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:41.520
to the 20+-episode-long seasons of "Star Trek:
The Next Generation."

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:46.430
The second season will end on May 5th, but
it's not going to be the end of "Picard" — Paramount

00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:50.240
worked back-to-back on Season 2 and the final,
third season.

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:52.450
So what does this mean for the adventure to
come?

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:57.689
Well, according to StarTrek.com, Q's final
trial will force the La Sirena crew to confront

00:01:57.689 --> 00:01:59.719
the perils of 21st-century Earth.

00:01:59.719 --> 00:02:04.680
It seems likely that this threat will bleed
into the third season in some way, considering

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:07.590
Paramount worked on both seasons at the same
time.

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:11.970
Showrunner Terry Matalas told SFX that there
are some "legendary moments" ahead.

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:17.260
It's worth noting that although "Picard" Season
2 starts airing on March 3rd, the third season

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:19.819
doesn't have an official release date just
yet.

00:02:19.819 --> 00:02:24.730
But Deadline previously reported that Paramount
is aiming for 2023.

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:29.440
Unsurprisingly, Patrick Stewart is coming
back to play Jean-Luc Picard, or should we

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:31.330
say, the synthetic version of Picard.

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:36.700
He'll also be joined by Santiago Cabrera,
who plays Cristóbal Rios, the captain of

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:38.030
the La Sirena.

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:42.739
Alison Pill will reprise her role as Dr. Agnes
Jurati, although thankfully, her scheming

00:02:42.739 --> 00:02:46.760
days are over after her heroic turn midway
through the first season.

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:51.780
Michelle Hurd is also coming back as Picard's
confidante, Raffi Musiker, while Isa Briones

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:54.599
will return as fellow synthetic-being Soji.

00:02:54.599 --> 00:02:59.510
Rounding out the La Sirena crew is Evan Evagora
as warrior monk Elnor.

00:02:59.510 --> 00:03:04.810
Original "Next Generation" star Brent Spiner
is also set to return as scientist Dr. Altan

00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:09.930
Inigo Soong, "Voyager" star Jeri Ryan will
step into Seven of Nine's shoes again, and

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:14.310
Orla Brady will continue to appear as Laris,
Picard's Romulan housekeeper.

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:18.270
But the guest stars of "Picard" Season 2 are
interesting too.

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:22.850
Whoopi Goldberg will play the diplomatic Guinan
again, offering counsel to Picard when he

00:03:22.850 --> 00:03:24.120
needs it most.

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:28.610
On the villain side of things, John de Lancie
will cause havoc for the gang as Q, and Annie

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:31.629
Wersching will replace Alice Krige as the
Borg Queen.

00:03:31.629 --> 00:03:34.349
Remember, Picard has dabbled with the Borg
in the past.

00:03:34.349 --> 00:03:37.840
He was assimilated into the hive as Locutus
of Borg.

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:43.260
So it'll be interesting to see how the Borg
Queen reacts to seeing him again after several decades.

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:49.680
Paramount+ released a full two-minute-long
look at "Picard" Season 2 on Star Trek Day 2021.

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:54.190
The footage shows Jean-Luc wandering around
his vineyard, reminiscing about his childhood

00:03:54.190 --> 00:03:58.799
days, while narration from Q explains that
the trial he subjected humanity to all the

00:03:58.799 --> 00:04:02.329
way back in "The Next Generation" season 1
never ended.

00:04:02.329 --> 00:04:06.800
From there, the action really gets going,
with Rios and the La Sirena crew realizing

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:12.060
that Q has changed the past so that the world
has been transformed into a "totalitarian nightmare."

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:18.210
It's a far cry away from the idealized future
of Starfleet and the Federation, that's for sure.

00:04:18.210 --> 00:04:21.880
The crew already knows that time's been altered
because Seven of Nine instantly realizes that

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:24.280
she has no Borg parts on her face anymore.

00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:27.920
Clearly, the fascist regime cut the Borg off
at the knees.

00:04:27.920 --> 00:04:32.420
The gang meets up with the Borg Queen, using
her technology to teleport to the past in

00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:34.930
an attempt to fix whatever Q did to the timeline.

00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:40.440
It's not exactly a new idea — Kirk and the
original Enterprise crew travel to the 20th

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:44.970
century in "Star Trek IV: The Voyage Home"
— but hopefully, the writers of "Picard"

00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:50.730
find inventive ways of pushing these characters
further than the typical fish-out-of-water comedy.

